  "text": "Question five. How do you handle late-arriving dimension members? Here an order for customer seventy-seven lands before customer seventy-seven does. Look up its key with an inner join, and the order simply vanishes: seven hundred dollars loaded, out of eleven hundred ten. No error, just a smaller total. Instead, insert a placeholder row for seventy-seven, its city unknown and flagged inferred, and load the order against that key. When the real customer arrives, update the placeholder in place. The order already points at it, so it reports Tulsa, and the fact table is never touched. One shared unknown row, key minus one, works too, but then every one of those facts has to be re-keyed later.\n\nQuestion six. What is a snapshot versus insert-only fact table strategy? Insert-only appends a row for every change: six rows for two orders, the whole history. A snapshot keeps one row per order, its state now. That's easy to query, but order five oh two just says cancelled. Nothing says it was paid first. The log can answer that: five oh two was paid, then cancelled. And it can rebuild the snapshot, by keeping the latest row per order. The snapshot can't rebuild the log. So when history has to be auditable, keep the insert-only log as the record, and serve the snapshot from it.",
